result.text:
$$
Y _ {k + 1} ^ {\mathrm{SE}} = Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{SE}} + f \big (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{SE}} \big) h + \sigma W _ {k} + f ^ {\prime} \big (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{SE}} \big) \Bigg (\sigma \underbrace {\left(\frac {1}{2} W _ {k} + H _ {k}\right) h} _ {= \int_ {t _ {k}} ^ {t _ {k + 1}} W _ {t _ {k}, r} d r} \Bigg) + O (h ^ {2}).
$$

Thus, the Shifted Euler scheme has a local error of $O ( h ^ { 2 } )$ whereas the Euler-Maruyama method produces a local error of $O ( h ^ { \frac { 3 } { 2 } } )$ , which is due to the integral highlighted above.

Remark 5.5. The shifted Euler method can be extended to time-varying difusion coeficients by replacing the terms σ $W _ { k }$ and $\begin{array} { r } { \sigma \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { t _ { k + 1 } } W _ { t _ { k } , r } d r } \end{array}$ with $\begin{array} { r } { \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { \tilde { t } _ { k + 1 } } \sigma ( r ) d W _ { r } } \end{array}$ and $\begin{array} { r } { \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { t _ { k + 1 } } \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { s } \sigma ( r ) d W _ { r } d s = \sigma ( t _ { k } ) \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { t _ { k + 1 } } W _ { t _ { k } , r } d r + \frac { \ddot { 1 } } { 6 } \big ( \sigma ( t _ { k + 1 } ) - \sigma ( t _ { k } ) \big ) \dot { W _ { k } } h ^ { 2 } + O \big ( h ^ { \frac { \gamma } { 2 } } \big ) } \end{array}$

Moreover, in applications where evaluating $f$ is significantly faster than generating Gaussian random vectors, we can remove the space-time L´evy area $H _ { k }$ from (5.8). The resulting “increment-only” Shifted Euler method will then have the same cost per step as the traditional Euler-Maruyama method. However, for suficiently small $h ,$ it will still be more accurate as it gives $\begin{array} { r } { \frac { 1 } { 2 } h W _ { k } \approx \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { t _ { k + 1 } } W _ { t _ { k } , r } } \end{array}$ dr in its Taylor expansion.

